<u>27-AUG-2005 11:53 PM (BKK time)</u><br>
Mr. Duty has been a huge help to us. He booked us on a tour that covers most of what we wanted to see.  We needed to be at the Landmark Hotel downtown to catch the tour.
<p>
Michelle had told me about how in BKK, they had no hot water as a standard, which initially worried me, but with the heat and humidity having a cold shower is all you need to feel refreshed (later on I found out that Mr. Duty has an electric water heater but I never used it).  We headed out via taxi. Mr. Duty was joining us to hang out downtown with his buddies in the "Jurassic Club" aptly named because everyone were dinorsaurs.
<p>
We actually had breakfast at an all-you-can-eat buffet at a 3-star hotel for $11 USD for 3 people.  We walked over to the Landmark Hotel and waited for the tour.
<p>
Today, we were going to Ayutthaya, an ancient city 2 hrs north of BKK and the 2nd capital of Thailand.  Ayutthaya is 745 years old and contains chedis (ancient palaces), and wat (temple) ruins.  The city itself is surrounded by water where 3 rivers meet, the Chao Praya bring the biggest.  
<p>
We were extremely lucky.  The tour operator arrived and told us we were the only ones on the tour.  We could stretch out and have a personalized tour.  I had great difficulty understanding our tour guide.  Her accent was thick and her English was very broken.  I whipped out my travelguide in a feeble attempt to tell her what I wanted to do in Thai.  I was totally butchering the words and not using the correct tones which are crucial in the Thai language.  I gave up!  It would be a complete struggle to communicate .  I just shook my head and agreed to whatever she said.  I have a feeling she was doing the same thing to me.  At times we did understand each other.
<p>
On the way to Ayutthaya, I noticed food vendors on the side of the highway and people on scooters riding on the shoulders against traffice.  Gas is about 23 baht/litre or very close to US prices (before I left).  
<p>
The first wat we arrived to was amazing.  Our tour guide did an excellent job of getting us involved.  Inside was a giant gold Buddha sitting.  It must have been 10 stories tall.  Inside, monks were blessing these orange colored cloths.  It was quite long (maybe 50m).  THen the cloth was thrown to the people standint on the Buddha statue.  The clothes were then draped around the giant statue and then thrown back to the people on the floor.  The worshippers on the floor then pulled the cloth over our heads.
<p>
