<p>
We then walked to the side of the Buddha, where our tour guide tried to explain to us to take a vase full of numbered sticks and shake them hard enough so that only one would fall out from the vigorous shaking.  This was one form of fortune telling used by Buddhists.    I got the number 4.  As far as I'm concerned, I'm a true believer.  The number 4 translated to me by the tour guide basically said that I was having difficulty finding the right person for me, but in Sept 2006, I will find her.  She will come from the East or the West.  I thought that was pretty damn good.  As for M, she got a 9 which meant that she would have a long life. However, if she wasn't married she wouldn't be.  Well thank goodness for her, right Dad :)
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1125336380_4.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1125336380_4.jpg"></a>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1125336380_4.jpg>-->
<p>
The next thing we did was take these two wooden pieces (one side was flat and the other side was rounded) and make a wish and throw them in the air.  The wish would come true if one of the peices landed on the flat side and one on the rounded side.  Neither M or I did good here.  So I guess we must be cursed or something.
<p> 
On our way out, we witnessed a Thai traditional dance being performed to appease Buddha (it was going to be one of many Thai dances we would see in a short period of time).  We got into the van and went to the next Wat which was a ruin.  It was called Wat Yai Chai Mongkol and contained the famous statue of the reclining Buddha.
<p>
The heat kicked in now and I was drenched in my own sweat and sticking to everything.  As I saw the giant reclining Buddha, it seemed odd to me. He looked like he was just relaxing on a couch and watching TV.  As I turned the corner, I saw the wat itself and the long and steep staircase ascending to the top of it.  Inside the wat was the ceremonial room with buddha in it and on the ceiling were maybe 50 bats.  Surrounding the wat where over a hundred buddha statues wrapped in yellow cloth. Quite a site. 
<p>
