<p> 
The heat started to really pour on so we grabbed a drink of some herbal concoction and some shade.  We decided to head out to lunch which was at an open-air restaurant on the Chao Praya river.  The food just seemed endless and way too much for two people.  4 authentic Thai dishes, plus soup, plus dessert.  We couldn't finish it.
<p>  
It was time to head to the last stop, Wat Phra Si Sanphet .  This wat considered of 3 chedis ruins, and several buddha statues where were beheaded by the Burmese who were at war during that time.  This popular lot was the biggest tourist attraction without Ayutthaya.  Lined with tour buses after tour buses, and school children, and tourists, it was croweded.  We encompassed all 3 chedis and decided to call it a day and head home.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1125338031_3.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1125338031_3.jpg"></a>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1125338031_3.jpg>-->
<p>
On the way home, we were taken to a jewelry store which happens to be something that every tour bus takes its tourist too.  We didn't really want any jewelry  and after a quick look, we left. 
<p>
We we got back to the Landmark Hotel, Mr. Duty was there waiting for us.  He had been kind enough to book us for the Thai Dance show and dinner.  We were already late when we arrived at the hotel so we hailed a cab and go to the location of the show.  As we walked in, seating was traditional Thai style where you sit on the floor with your legs crossed and eat from a low table.  The Thai dance itself, came after dinner, and while it is a nice dance to watch and the explosion of colors from their outfits is mesmerizing, I still have to say that I find India's Bharat Natayam dance to be much more involved and frankly better.  That' s not a knock on Thai dancing at all. It's quite graceful and beautiful and certainly worth watching.
<p>
